00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:17,920
Why don't we like, well, don't you want to try something new?

37
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:21,320
No, I do not want to try anything new.

38
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:25,080
Well, like the things you're doing seem so great, you seem pretty grumpy.

39
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:28,000
Maybe it's because you don't try new fun things.

40
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,560
No, I don't want to be grumpy.

41
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:34,480
All right.

42
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:35,480
So moving on.

43
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:39,760
All right, so selfishness and cooperation.

44
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:44,120
Peer theorists very rarely mention selfishness, or in fact, I've heard many peer theorists

45
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:50,640
actually deny flatly that economics has any assumption of selfishness baked into it.

46
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:55,720
But almost all real world applications of economic theory do assume narrowly selfish

47
00:02:55,720 --> 00:02:56,720
behavior.

48
00:02:56,720 --> 00:02:58,800
This is actually what's going on.

49
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:00,640
This is why we caught Adam Smith.

50
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:05,360
When we say socialism just doesn't work, whenever we say like incentives matter, tragedy of

51
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:10,320
the commons, whenever we talk about rent control, whenever we talk about taxes, underlying all

52
00:03:10,320 --> 00:03:14,800
of this is the idea that people don't like working for strangers for free.

53
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:17,840
People don't like just giving stuff to strangers.

54
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:21,560
People don't want to suffer just because it benefits society.

55
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:24,600
This is, there are a few people like that maybe.

56
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:27,400
There's a lot of people who claim to be like that who aren't.

57
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:31,440
And there's a whole lot of people who just grump and are, no, I don't want to.

58
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:33,040
All right.

59
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:37,240
So we have almost all real world applications assume that people are narrowly selfish.

60
00:03:37,240 --> 00:03:42,880
Now a slight variation on this, which a number of economists have picked up from the field

61
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:53,480
of evolutionary psychology, is something called inclusive fitness.

62
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:56,120
Right now, how many of you have ever heard of a book called The Selfish Gene by Richard

63
00:03:56,120 --> 00:03:57,120
Dawkins?

64
00:03:57,120 --> 00:03:58,120
Yes.

65
00:03:58,120 --> 00:04:01,440
So that is probably still the best popularization of the idea of inclusive fitness.

66
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:10,240
So the idea here is that human beings, though we love being alive and doing all of our stuff,

67
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:11,800
but also we like all other animals.

68
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:14,160
We are here because of our genes.

69
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:19,560
And genes that don't propagate themselves tend to die out.

70
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:23,280
We, the very fact that we're here indicates we got the kind of genes that haven't died

71
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:24,280
out so far.

72
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:25,280
We've made it so far.

73
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:26,280
Right?

74
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:31,600
And now when you start thinking about this, there are many ways this just simply, this

75
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:32,600
predicts narrow selfishness.

76
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:38,960
Like, so, I suppose there were cavemen a million years ago who went and gave all their food

77
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:43,320
away to total strangers with no expectation of ever being paid back.

78
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:45,800
What's likely to happen to cavemen like that?

79
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:46,920
They die.

80
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:49,600
So we are not their descendants.

81
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:53,120
We're the descendants of the cavemen who did not like to go and throw and give their food

82
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:54,920
away to total strangers for free.

83
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:57,040
All right, pretty obvious point.

84
00:04:57,040 --> 00:05:01,200
Now, Inclusive Fitness goes and adds on extra insight that says, yeah, but suppose a million

85
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:06,320
years ago there was a caveman who looked at his baby and said, you little beggar, what

86
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:07,920
are you ever going to do for me?

87
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:09,600
Well, you're like, you can't even talk.

88
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:12,400
You're not, you won't even remember what I've ever done for you.

89
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:15,200
And I could easily be dead by the time you're old enough to help me out.

90
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:16,560
So forget you, little baby.

91
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:22,320
All right, so imagine that there were some cavemen who treated their own biological offspring

92
00:05:22,320 --> 00:05:25,040
with that level of apathy.

93
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:30,760
All right, well, what happened to those babies?

94
00:05:30,760 --> 00:05:31,760
They died off.

95
00:05:31,760 --> 00:05:33,880
So how many descendants did those cavemen have?

96
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:34,880
None.

97
00:05:34,880 --> 00:05:38,160
So we are not descended from those cavemen either.

98
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:42,080
We're descended from the cavemen who didn't like giving stuff to total strangers for free

99
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:44,600
but love their own blood.

100
00:05:44,600 --> 00:05:46,760
Love their own blood relations.

101
00:05:46,760 --> 00:05:49,600
Now how much do they need to love their own blood relations?

102
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:55,360
This is where inclusive fitness gets interesting because there's a whole set of fractions associated

103
00:05:55,360 --> 00:05:58,280
with the degree of blood relation.

104
00:05:58,280 --> 00:06:03,880
So for example, with a biological sibling or a biological child, what fraction of your

105
00:06:03,880 --> 00:06:05,440
genes do you approximately share?

106
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:06,440
One.

107
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:07,440
One half.

108
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:08,440
One half.

109
00:06:08,440 --> 00:06:10,960
Yes, one half.

110
00:06:10,960 --> 00:06:15,320
On the other hand, with say a cousin, how many, so if you have a first cousin, what

111
00:06:15,320 --> 00:06:20,640
fraction of your genes do you have in common with your first cousin?

112
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:21,640
An eighth.

113
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:22,640
An eighth.

114
00:06:22,640 --> 00:06:31,480
Yes, because remember that, so, so with your own, so if with your brother, say, so I have

115
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:36,360
half my brother's genes and his daughter has half of his genes, so my niece has one fourth

116
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:40,800
of my genes, but my kids only have half of mine, so they actually only have an eighth

117
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:41,800
each.

118
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:43,240
All right.

119
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:44,240
And so on.

120
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:46,280
So you can go and crunch all this math.

121
00:06:46,280 --> 00:06:50,440
It's actually more complicated in the real world because there's actually mild incest

122
00:06:50,440 --> 00:06:52,560
going on all the time.

123
00:06:52,560 --> 00:06:56,780
So for example, if you marry within your own ethnicity, then you probably actually share

124
00:06:56,780 --> 00:07:02,080
more than half the genes with your kids because you are a distant relation of your spouse.

125
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:03,080
All right.

126
00:07:03,080 --> 00:07:06,720
So I don't want to gross anybody out, but that's what's going on.

127
00:07:06,720 --> 00:07:07,720
All right.

128
00:07:07,720 --> 00:07:11,800
Now, this led, well, I think it was actually the founder of inclusive fitness theory, Hamilton,

129
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:15,200
and he was asked, would you die for your brother, he said, no, but I would die for two brothers

130
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:17,200
or eight cousins.

131
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:19,480
All right.

132
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:25,360
Now that sounds a little bit stronger than reality and support, but again, the idea of

133
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:30,160
this is that when we are evaluating human selfishness, we should be looking at it not

134
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:35,800
through the narrow view of the, of the interests of the individual, but rather through this,

135
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:39,040
rather through the genes eye point of view, because that's really what we're evolved to

136
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:40,780
be like.

